First, we observe that, since we have an equivalence \[ [{\cal C}^{	extup{op}}, { f Set }]\slash P \simeq [({	extstyle \int P})^{	extup{op}}, { f Set }] \] (see, for instance, Proposition A1.1.7 \cite{El}) induced (by composition with the Yoneda embedding ${\int P}	o [({\int P})^{	extup{op}}, { f Set }]$) by the canonical functor $G_{P}:\int P	o [{\cal C}^{	extup{op}}, { f Set }]\slash P$ (sending any object $(c, x)\in \int P$ to the object $y_{\cal C}(c)	o P$ of $[{\cal C}^{	extup{op}}, { f Set }]\slash P$ given by the arrow corresponding to $x$ via the Yoneda Lemma), $G_{P}$ is flat. Now, $F_{P}$ is the composite of $G_{P}$ with the inverse image of the geometric morphism ${ f Sh}({\cal C}, J)\slash a_{J}(P) 	o [{\cal C}^{	extup{op}}, { f Set }]\slash P$ induced by the canonical geometric inclusion ${ f Sh}({\cal C}, J)\hookrightarrow [{\cal C}^{	extup{op}}, { f Set }]$ as in Example ef{exgeometricinclusion}, so it is flat as $G_{P}$ is. Notice that the Grothendieck topology $J_{P}$ is precisely the topology induced by $F_{P}$ (in the sense of Proposition ef{reminducedtopology}). Let us apply Corollary ef{corflatequivalence2} to prove that $F_{P}$ induces an equivalence. First, we have to show that the objects in the image of the functor $F_{P}$ define a separating set of objects for the topos ${ f Sh}({\cal C}, J)\slash a_{J}(P)$. Given two distinct arrows $\alpha,  eta:(\gamma:Q	o a_{J}(P))	o (\xi:Q'	o a_{J}(P))$ in ${ f Sh}({\cal C}, J)\slash a_{J}(P)$, we have to show that there are an object $c$ of $\cal C$, an element $x\in P(c)$, corresponding to an arrow $P_{x}:l(c)	o a_{J}(P)$ in ${ f Sh}({\cal C}, J)$, and an element $y\in Q(c)$, corresponding to an arrow $\overline{y}:l(c)	o Q$ in ${ f Sh}({\cal C}, J)$, such that $P_{x}=\gamma \circ \overline{y}$ and $\alpha\circ \overline{y}
eq  eta\circ \overline{y}$. Consider the pullback $	ilde{\gamma}:	ilde{Q}	o P$ of $\gamma:Q	o a_{J}(P)$ along the unit $P	o a_{J}(P)$. Since the canonical arrow $z:	ilde{Q}	o Q$ is sent by $a_{J}$ to an isomorphism and $a_{J}$ is left adjoint to the inclusion functor, $\alpha\circ z
eq  eta \circ z$. So there exists an object $c$ of $\cal C$ and an element $y'\in 	ilde{Q}(c)$ such that $\alpha(z(c)(y'))
eq  eta(z(c)(y'))$. So, posing $x=	ilde{\gamma}(c)(y')$ and $y=z(c)(y')$, we obtain that $P_{x}=\gamma \circ \overline{y}$ and $\alpha\circ \overline{y}
eq  eta\circ \overline{y}$, as required. It now remains to show that condition (ii) is satisfied by $F_{P}$; but this is clear, since, for any object $(c, x)$ of $\int P$, any subobject $A$ of the object $P_{x}:l(c)	o a_{J}(P)$ in ${ f Sh}({\cal C}, J)\slash a_{J}(P)$ corresponds to a subobject of $l(c)$ in ${ f Sh}({\cal C}, J)$ and hence to a sieve $S$ on $c$, which clearly lifts to a sieve $S_{\pi}$ on the object $(c, x)$ in $\int P$ such that $A$ is isomorphic to the union of the images of the arrows $F_{P}(f)$ where $f\in S_{\pi}$.

Given two distinct arrows $\alpha,  eta:(\gamma:Q	o a_{J}(P))	o (\xi:Q'	o a_{J}(P))$ in ${ f Sh}({\cal C}, J)\slash a_{J}(P)$, we have to show that there are an object $c$ of $\cal C$, an element $x\in P(c)$, corresponding to an arrow $P_{x}:l(c)	o a_{J}(P)$ in ${ f Sh}({\cal C}, J)$, and an element $y\in Q(c)$, corresponding to an arrow $\overline{y}:l(c)	o Q$ in ${ f Sh}({\cal C}, J)$, such that $P_{x}=\gamma \circ \overline{y}$ and $\alpha\circ \overline{y}
eq  eta\circ \overline{y}$. Consider the pullback $	ilde{\gamma}:	ilde{Q}	o P$ of $\gamma:Q	o a_{J}(P)$ along the unit $P	o a_{J}(P)$. Since the canonical arrow $z:	ilde{Q}	o Q$ is sent by $a_{J}$ to an isomorphism and $a_{J}$ is left adjoint to the inclusion functor, $\alpha\circ z
eq  eta \circ z$. So there exists an object $c$ of $\cal C$ and an element $y'\in 	ilde{Q}(c)$ such that $\alpha(z(c)(y'))
eq  eta(z(c)(y'))$. So, posing $x=	ilde{\gamma}(c)(y')$ and $y=z(c)(y')$, we obtain that $P_{x}=\gamma \circ \overline{y}$ and $\alpha\circ \overline{y}
eq  eta\circ \overline{y}$, as required.
